5846	\N	Substance use was common in persons at-risk for and diagnosed with HIV in a trial of F/TAF for prep (Discover)	Background: DISCOVER is an ongoing randomized controlled trial comparing emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide to emtricitabine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate for HIV pre‐exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) in cisgender men who have sex with men and transgender women. We assessed associations between recreational drug use and incident human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) within DISCOVER. Methods: During DISCOVER, recreational drug use was self‐reported using confidential computer‐aided selfinterview. We compared drug use in the three months prior to study screening for the 22 persons who acquired HIV to the 5,365 persons who remained uninfected at the primary endpoint (when 100% of participants had reached week 48 and 50% had reached week 96). P values comparing recreational drug use between participant groups were calculated using the Cochran‐Mantel‐Haenszel test. Results: The median age of participants in DISCOVER was 34 years (range 18‐76); 9% were Black and 24% were Hispanic or Latinx. At screening, 67% of participants reported recreational drug use in the preceding three months. The most common recreational drugs reportedly used were amyl nitrate (46%), cannabis (40%), Viagra (23%), cocaine (23%), Ecstasy (18%), and gamma hydroxybutyrate (17%). Overall, self‐reported recreational drug use was similar between participants who did and did not acquire HIV (p=0.55). Recreational drug use was not associated with low study drug adherence (odds ratio of low adherence for those with no recreational drug use versus those with use 1.54, 95% CI 0.92‐2.60). However, when compared to those who did not seroconvert, participants who acquired HIV infection were more likely to report use of methamphetamine (p<0.001), codeine (p=0.003), mephedrone (p=0.019), or crack cocaine (p=0.045). Conclusions: Recreational drug use was common in participants taking PrEP in DISCOVER, with a wide array of drugs being reported. While recreational drug use overall was not associated with HIV acquisition, participants who acquired HIV were more likely to report the use of methamphetamine, mephedrone, crack cocaine, or codeine.	Substance use was common in persons at-risk for and diagnosed with HIV in a trial of F/TAF for prep (Discover).^\nBackground: DISCOVER is an ongoing randomized controlled trial comparing emtricitabine/tenofovir alafenamide to emtricitabine/tenofovir disoproxil fumarate for HIV pre‐exposure prophylaxis (PrEP) in cisgender men who have sex with men and transgender women. We assessed associations between recreational drug use and incident human immunodeficiency virus (HIV) within DISCOVER. Methods: During DISCOVER, recreational drug use was self‐reported using confidential computer‐aided selfinterview. We compared drug use in the three months prior to study screening for the 22 persons who acquired HIV to the 5,365 persons who remained uninfected at the primary endpoint (when 100% of participants had reached week 48 and 50% had reached week 96). P values comparing recreational drug use between participant groups were calculated using the Cochran‐Mantel‐Haenszel test. Results: The median age of participants in DISCOVER was 34 years (range 18‐76); 9% were Black and 24% were Hispanic or Latinx. At screening, 67% of participants reported recreational drug use in the preceding three months. The most common recreational drugs reportedly used were amyl nitrate (46%), cannabis (40%), Viagra (23%), cocaine (23%), Ecstasy (18%), and gamma hydroxybutyrate (17%). Overall, self‐reported recreational drug use was similar between participants who did and did not acquire HIV (p=0.55). Recreational drug use was not associated with low study drug adherence (odds ratio of low adherence for those with no recreational drug use versus those with use 1.54, 95% CI 0.92‐2.60). However, when compared to those who did not seroconvert, participants who acquired HIV infection were more likely to report use of methamphetamine (p<0.001), codeine (p=0.003), mephedrone (p=0.019), or crack cocaine (p=0.045). Conclusions: Recreational drug use was common in participants taking PrEP in DISCOVER, with a wide array of drugs being reported. While recreational drug use overall was not associated with HIV acquisition, participants who acquired HIV were more likely to report the use of methamphetamine, mephedrone, crack cocaine, or codeine.	['*Human immunodeficiency virus infection', '*substance use', 'Adult', 'Clinical trial', 'Conference abstract', 'Controlled study', 'Drug abuse', 'Female', 'Hispanic', 'Human', 'Major clinical study', 'Male', 'Mantel Haenszel test', 'Medication compliance', 'Pre‐exposure prophylaxis']	\N	2020		\N	\N	1
9545	24613031	Profiles of psychiatric symptoms among amphetamine type stimulant and ketamine using inpatients in Wuhan, China	Amphetamine type stimulants (ATS) and ketamine have emerged as major drug problems in China, and chronic extensive exposure to these substances frequently co-occurs with psychiatric symptoms. This study compares the psychiatric symptoms of patients reporting ATS use only, ATS and ketamine use, or ketamine use only who were admitted to an inpatient psychiatry ward in Wuhan, China between 2010 and 2011. Data on 375 study participants collected during their ward admission and extracted from their clinical records included their socio-demographics, scores on the Brief Psychiatric Rating Scale (BPRS), and urine toxicology screens. Results: The ketamine-only group had significantly lower total BPRS scores and significantly lower scores on Thinking Disorder, Activity, and Hostility-Suspicion BPRS subscales than the ATS-only and ATS+ketamine groups (p<0.001 for all comparisons). The ketamine-only group also had significantly higher scores on the subscales of Anxiety-Depression and Anergia. The ATS-only group had significantly higher scores on subscales of Thinking Disorder, Activity, and Hostility-Suspicion and significantly lower scores on Anxiety-Depression and Anergia subscales than the ketamine-only and ATS+ketamine groups (p<0.001 for all comparisons). A K-means cluster method identified three distinct clusters of patients based on the similarities of their BPRS subscale profiles, and the identified clusters differed markedly on the proportions of participants reporting different primary drugs of abuse. The study findings suggest that ketamine and ATS users present with different profiles of psychiatric symptoms at admission to inpatient treatment. © 2014 Elsevier Ltd.	Profiles of psychiatric symptoms among amphetamine type stimulant and ketamine using inpatients in Wuhan, China.^\nAmphetamine type stimulants (ATS) and ketamine have emerged as major drug problems in China, and chronic extensive exposure to these substances frequently co-occurs with psychiatric symptoms. This study compares the psychiatric symptoms of patients reporting ATS use only, ATS and ketamine use, or ketamine use only who were admitted to an inpatient psychiatry ward in Wuhan, China between 2010 and 2011. Data on 375 study participants collected during their ward admission and extracted from their clinical records included their socio-demographics, scores on the Brief Psychiatric Rating Scale (BPRS), and urine toxicology screens. Results: The ketamine-only group had significantly lower total BPRS scores and significantly lower scores on Thinking Disorder, Activity, and Hostility-Suspicion BPRS subscales than the ATS-only and ATS+ketamine groups (p<0.001 for all comparisons). The ketamine-only group also had significantly higher scores on the subscales of Anxiety-Depression and Anergia. The ATS-only group had significantly higher scores on subscales of Thinking Disorder, Activity, and Hostility-Suspicion and significantly lower scores on Anxiety-Depression and Anergia subscales than the ketamine-only and ATS+ketamine groups (p<0.001 for all comparisons). A K-means cluster method identified three distinct clusters of patients based on the similarities of their BPRS subscale profiles, and the identified clusters differed markedly on the proportions of participants reporting different primary drugs of abuse. The study findings suggest that ketamine and ATS users present with different profiles of psychiatric symptoms at admission to inpatient treatment. © 2014 Elsevier Ltd.	['amphetamine derivative', 'central stimulant agent', 'ketamine', 'adult', 'article', 'China', 'drug abuse', 'female', 'hospital patient', 'hostility', 'human', 'major clinical study', 'male', 'mental disease', 'mixed anxiety and depression', 'priority journal', 'psychological rating scale', 'suspiciousness', 'thought disorder']	10.1016/j.jpsychires.2014.02.010	2014	